WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.860
A boy and his blob, trouble in Blobolonia.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:27.600
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.680
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:30.860
And I'm Joe.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.980
It's a silly title, but I can't be the only one getting Steven Spielberg vibes from this

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:42.660
game with that Indiana Jones font on the main menu and even maybe some ripoff music from

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:44.200
Indiana Jones as well in the beginning.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.480
But it's also like a kid teaming up.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.020
I don't think that's Spielberg. Spielberg is Indiana Jones.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:50.880
Well, it's George Lucas, right?

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.240
But I think he was also, I think Spielberg was also.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.480
Yeah, he was attached, but like, I don't know.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.460
Forget the whole Indiana Jones thing for a second.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.840
The whole like kid teams up with an alien like creature and sets out on a journey bigger than

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.840
it first appears. Like, that's a Spielberg thing.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:06.220
It's very Spielberg.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.040
It's actually crazy to me that I hadn't thought of that before because the first thought I had

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.520
was like, wow, those first few notes of that song is the Indiana Jones theme.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:22.020
What I was thinking is more a boy and his dog, which is a post-apocalyptic pastiche of society.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:28.860
It's very mean spirited and it has absolutely nothing to do with the video game, but it rhymes.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.440
It rhymes. It's like poetry.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:33.080
It rhymes. Yeah.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.320
There you go. That's your George Lucas reference.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.800
Yeah. Every frame is so dense.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.560
That's a deep cut.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:48.640
A boy and his blob is a puzzle platformer where you only play as the boy who must help the

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.080
computer blob save Blobolonia.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:57.440
Blob can transform into many useful tools and shapes depending on the jelly beans the boy

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.940
feeds him. And I feel like if I leave it at just that, that's funny enough to get us started because

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.200
David Crane, who wants you to know he made this game, it's called David Crane's A Boy

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:10.520
and His Blob on the main menu.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:18.640
That is a unique start of an inception for an idea of just, you know, I think that this boy

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.700
is going to feed this alien blob jelly beans and that's how he'll change into other shapes.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:30.620
Yeah, there was definitely some kind of drug experience that preceded the creation of this

00:02:30.620 --> 00:02:37.200
game to put the jelly beans and the space blobs and the boy himself together.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:43.500
And again, I just want to state that in the manual, you will just hear references to your

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:47.340
main character as just the boy, which I think is hilarious.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:54.320
And I don't know why, but yeah, it's, it's very off the wall.

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:57.660
It's a very unique setting that we've been put into here.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:03.220
And for the jelly beans, like that's the core mechanic of this game, the jelly beans and

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.980
using them appropriately to transform the blob into whatever you need.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:14.800
So though the player directly controls the boy and Blobber, as I think he's called, I've

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:17.740
been calling him blob, not his full name.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.040
He's controlled by the computer.

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:24.260
So depending on what jelly bean you feed him, let's say it's a licorice jelly bean.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.640
He changes into a ladder.

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.920
And apparently, according to the manual, the flavors are named specifically as either puns

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.060
or alliteration to help the players remember them.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:41.100
The licorice one, I guess I get like, um, it, it, the, I don't know why I'm thinking

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:44.980
like snakes and ladders for some reason, but like twizzlers and some of them are better

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.400
than others. Just the alliteration.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:52.960
But yeah, before even reading the manual, it was like, it was just really fun to discover

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:59.300
that, you know, to be like, uh, you know, like tangerine turns into trampoline.

00:03:59.540 --> 00:04:03.460
It just like, it just, they kind of sound the same or like the punch punch.

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:07.660
Yeah. The punch jelly bean makes a hole because you punch a hole like that.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.580
That was fun. And it made it like a little, you know, light puzzle element.

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:14.820
And they want you to discover what each jelly bean does, right?

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:16.640
Like it's not spelled out for you.

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.260
I don't, I don't think it's in the manual.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:19.440
Could anybody confirm that?

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:21.200
I didn't read it.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.900
So I was learning as I was playing.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.920
I did eventually read it.

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:29.360
And, uh, it is not, it does not tell you what does what in the manual.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.660
Right. So it's all experiment.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.540
Yeah, exactly. It's all about experimenting.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:37.580
However, you know, and we can, uh, I think we should just talk about the transformations

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:38.660
and their usefulness.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:43.640
Um, it's, uh, it's not really up to experimentation, right?

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:50.860
Like you, you need a very specific jelly bean to do a very specific thing each time.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:56.340
Uh, you can't like get creative and say like, oh, to get to the next platform, like I'll use

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.500
the umbrella and fly.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.000
Like, no, you, you need the ladder to climb up to the next platform.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:06.020
Uh, so there's always like a specific answer.

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:12.800
Yeah. I think that it is pretty, um, what's the word I'm looking for?

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:18.800
There isn't a lot of freedom, but I can imagine that some of these can be solved in several

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:25.240
ways. Like, especially if it's like a ladder or a trampoline, I'm sure that like they can be sort

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:32.340
of used either or, but it's unlike a lot of games that we've played that have this, have

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:42.100
a more strict illusion of, of choice and an expanding, uh, like inventory where like, while, you know,

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:49.280
playing with the umbrella might not actually work for like the, the specific puzzle you're

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.200
trying to solve. You can still play around with the umbrella.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:57.920
Like you can still, uh, jump around on a trampoline, even if it's not in a place where a trampoline

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.300
is exactly what you need.

00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:05.660
Like you can still do these things and in, it's at least a step above a lot of games that

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:09.720
we played where the abilities are more like key items than anything else.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:15.540
Yeah. That's a good point that, uh, it's not, um, a location dependent that like if you fed

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:19.280
blob a licorice and he's like, this isn't the area where I need to be a ladder.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.460
So nothing happens. It's like, he'll just turn into the ladder and it could be completely useless to you.

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:29.760
Yeah. There were times I like, I think, I think it's safe to say the intended use of the umbrella

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.740
is to like jump off of something and not have fall damage.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:38.940
Yeah. But then, um, but there are times where there's like rocks falling and you could just

00:06:38.940 --> 00:06:41.980
dodge those rocks like you would think to do in any video game, but you can also use the

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.200
umbrella there to protect yourself from the rocks.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.440
Like there are a couple of things like that that notice, but even with the intended, um,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:54.940
like methods of using or solving each little puzzle, like it still feels like you're being

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.480
creative. Like, yes, they, they came up with like, this is probably what you should do here, but

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:04.560
you still have to use your like creative, uh, problem solving to decide like, okay, what

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.420
do I know about what each of these jelly beans do?

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:08.140
And like, which one could maybe help me?

00:07:08.240 --> 00:07:11.600
Like there's a, there's a part where you're trying to get back up to the surface.

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.360
Cause a lot of the game takes place in like the sewer and underground and the sewer grade

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:21.100
is still there. And there's an apple jelly bean that turns it into a, like a car jack, an apple jack.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:25.920
And, uh, you know, that one took me a while, but it was satisfying to be like, oh yeah,

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:29.980
I can like knock this, um, this lid off of the sewer.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:36.800
Yeah. I'm not going to give a one-to-one comparison to portal, but like even portal with the amount

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:42.900
of freedom that that allowed you, like certain concepts were the solve.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:48.600
Like once you had that, you solved the puzzle and it wasn't like there were 15 different

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.860
ways to do it.

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:56.320
Like a lot of puzzle games as, as much freedom as they make you think you have, they are still

00:07:56.320 --> 00:07:58.260
kind of one-to-one.

00:07:58.460 --> 00:08:02.960
So is it fun to then like feed jelly beans to this blob?

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:07.240
And then, cause remember your character can only walk left and right.

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.100
Like your player can't jump.

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:10.600
They can't swim on their own.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:15.500
If they fall from more than like, I think it's actually one and a half screens, anything

00:08:15.500 --> 00:08:17.120
beyond that, they'll die.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:23.320
Uh, so they really need blob to get around, but is it fun to call blob over via whistle,

00:08:23.740 --> 00:08:30.080
have him stand in a specific spot, feed a jelly bean, and then test out, uh, you know, what

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:31.420
you think can work?

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:32.960
I say that it is.

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:40.180
I mean, I think that it's very, um, it could get repetitive if the game were longer, but

00:08:40.180 --> 00:08:45.220
for the most part, until like the, towards the end of like the phase one of this game

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:50.780
where you're, where you're just collecting money, I was mostly not, I did not get like

00:08:50.780 --> 00:08:52.540
tired of the loop.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:55.160
Towards the end, there was a couple of times, cause I was on the last couple of treasures

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:59.400
that I was, you know, by nature of hunting down specific treasures by the end, it's going

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:01.200
to be harder and harder to find cause there's only a few left.

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.180
So like, then I was getting like a little worn out of, of the gameplay loop, but for

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:10.300
the most part, I feel like it, that part of the gameplay loop ended at exactly the perfect

00:09:10.300 --> 00:09:14.280
time where I wasn't tired of it because I had always been discovering something new up

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:15.700
until the end of that.

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:24.200
I also like just, uh, on a more general level, the, the companion nature of the blob.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.460
Like he's kind of an idiot.

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.480
Like he looks so dumb.

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.020
Like he's, he's, he really is dumb.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.460
He looks like a snowman.

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:33.800
Like, yeah, yeah. But like, just look at his face.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.000
It's dumb. And the, yeah, yeah, but it is your friend.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:39.160
He's your dumb friend.

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.140
And you'll, you'll be like, come here, like, come here, get over here.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:43.980
You'll whistle him over.

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.220
And it's not like, it's not perfect.

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:47.120
It takes a minute.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:48.680
He's slower than you.

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:54.160
And then he does his dumb bounce over to you and he'll get like too close and like,

00:09:54.220 --> 00:09:55.080
Hey, give me some space.

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.320
I need to throw this at you.

00:09:56.460 --> 00:10:02.180
And then he'll, I mean, like, these are things that should be very annoying, but because he's

00:10:02.180 --> 00:10:08.360
just your dumb blob friend, it makes it, uh, kind of special and it's very endearing.

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:12.120
And so I think that it really milked that.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:18.120
But especially when you consider this, you know, setting and, uh, the type of gameplay

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:23.640
that, uh, a boy and his blob sets out for you, this would be a tonally weird game.

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:30.780
If, uh, you were fighting enemies often and needed like blobs assistance in beating them

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:31.620
up or something.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:37.080
But for the, you know, mostly the, there's very limited amount of enemies, uh, uh, like

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:39.720
the hopping snakes and falling rocks and stuff like that.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:41.480
That's stuff you have to avoid.

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:44.240
It's not about killing them or anything like that.

00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:51.180
And so for the most part, it's this fun platforming, uh, reminds me almost of, uh, the original

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:55.960
Prince of Persia, you know, where it's like, you don't have any cool weapons or anything.

00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:02.340
It's just about figuring out how to get to the next area or, you know, via exploration,

00:11:02.940 --> 00:11:08.160
figuring out like, what do I need to do in order to get over to that next screen?

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:13.780
And that's like, that's the part of the experimentation is, is that there isn't an open, you know,

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:17.800
yeah, there's some secrets and stuff, but there isn't an open endedness to what you can do.

00:11:18.060 --> 00:11:23.860
Mostly, you know, when you need to transform blob into something because it's just that there's

00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:28.220
a, you know, you're down in the caves and there's a gap between there, the next two platforms.

00:11:28.220 --> 00:11:33.400
And so, you know, that you need to turn him into the like bridge that gaps between them,

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:35.600
or it's a little bit of an uneven thing.

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:41.380
So you've got to maybe try to get up to another platform and use the hole to fall through to get

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:42.340
to the other side.

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:46.120
Like it's not, it's not guesswork, I guess is what I'm saying.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.060
You don't have to just assume that there's a puzzle everywhere.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:51.200
You'll know when they present themselves.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:56.460
On top of that, though, it's not guesswork for sure, but there are built in secrets to

00:11:56.460 --> 00:12:01.160
where like, usually they're not only unless I just didn't find any, I don't think there

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:05.040
are any that we've seen in other games where it's like hit this specific tile of the wall

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.080
that you would never be able to see otherwise.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:10.620
But it's like, oh, there's a ceiling above me.

00:12:10.700 --> 00:12:13.620
And then somewhere up there, there's a little gap and like, hey, I wonder if I can use a

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:16.560
trampoline up there and like you might just get a reward for going up there.

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:24.860
Yeah, I appreciate that they were, it was more intuitive than some game that was less

00:12:24.860 --> 00:12:31.680
creative in the, in like the general design that made it like, all right, now find, yeah,

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:38.660
find the spot and you're just going to have to have your bouncing buddy just go from pixel

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:40.020
to pixel searching for it.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:45.780
And I think the playfulness of the transformations, it varies.

00:12:45.780 --> 00:12:52.360
So like the bridge and the ladder are just very specific uses that once you figure them

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:54.600
out the first time, you'll know when you need them.

00:12:54.760 --> 00:12:59.020
But the trampoline is something cool where like you bounce on it once, you get like a

00:12:59.020 --> 00:13:02.440
little hop. And then if you just stay there, it's like the next hop's even bigger.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:05.780
And the hop after that, it's like, it gets really almost an infinite.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:09.660
Yeah. It's like comedic how, how high you jump up.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:14.800
And I think, you know, more of the transformations could have been like that, but the, you know,

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:15.980
what are you going to do with the ladder?

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:18.640
I suppose to make it a comic book.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:24.220
Yeah. I mean, just the nature of him trancing form, transforming, trancing form, transforming into

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.700
a ladder is just silly enough for that.

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.240
But yeah, I mean, cause there are, what, how many are there?

00:13:28.300 --> 00:13:29.240
How many transformations are there?

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:31.400
There's a lot. There's like, there's like 15 or something.

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:32.340
At least a dozen. Yeah.

00:13:32.720 --> 00:13:37.620
There's a lot. And I'm, to this day, I'm still trying to figure out the connection between strawberry and bridge.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.520
Me too. That's like the only one I don't know.

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:41.620
Strawberry bridges forever.

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:43.080
That's what I was thinking actually.

00:13:43.080 --> 00:13:45.220
Is there a bridge in strawberry fields forever?

00:13:47.720 --> 00:13:52.820
Cause otherwise I'm out of luck here, but, uh, Applejack, like I could, give me that all

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:53.920
day. Yeah.

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:55.560
Root beer rocket. It was cool.

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:57.460
Right. Coke, uh, cola bubble.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:00.380
I get it. And the ketchup jelly bean.

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:01.480
We talk about that for a second.

00:14:01.740 --> 00:14:02.580
Yeah. That's a cool one.

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:04.100
Go for it. Yeah. It took me a while.

00:14:04.180 --> 00:14:05.560
Cause that's the only one he won't eat.

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.300
So I was like, okay, maybe this comes in the late game.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.140
You gotta use it. You gotta get, you gotta unlock something before he'll eat it or something.

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:16.760
But, uh, I do wish the manual was a little less on the nose with this because it would

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:17.740
have been really cool to figure out.

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:22.080
But when I read the manual, it does say like, um, you know, the, the only, the only one,

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:24.840
uh, you won't eat is the ketchup jelly bean.

00:14:24.940 --> 00:14:28.460
And then later on it says like, be careful because if you like jump down several screens and

00:14:28.460 --> 00:14:32.580
the blob can't get to you, you know, you're going to have to find a way for him to catch

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:34.480
up, but it spelled it like ketchup.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.880
I think it would have been cool if it just spelled it like catch up.

00:14:37.140 --> 00:14:38.200
And then you have to figure it out yourself.

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:39.540
Like that's a cool one.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:44.180
Like I, so if you throw a ketchup jelly bean when the blob is not like near you, he will

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:45.620
just appear there. He'll catch up to you.

00:14:45.780 --> 00:14:47.280
But isn't that weird since he hates it?

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.140
Yeah. Maybe he's barking at it.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:54.000
Maybe he's like, he's like, ah, I saw somebody laying a ketchup jelly bean down there.

00:14:54.420 --> 00:14:59.820
Yeah. Um, and the other one that I found cool that serves a similar purpose, if you, if you climb,

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:07.700
if you trampoline your way up like many screens, um, you can use the honey jelly bean to turn

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:10.760
him into a hummingbird and the hummingbird can fly.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:11.620
So he'll fly up to you.

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:14.140
But like, it's just so there, I just wasn't expecting.

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:18.520
I mean, I'm up at one point seven or eight screens above the blob.

